(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-10"] = {"phy-11-10-m01":{"idea":"A semiconductor conducts better than an insulator and worse than a metal.","steps":["Conductors have very high conductivity.","Insulators have almost none.","Semiconductors like silicon and germanium lie in between, and impurity, heat or light can change it."],"others":"It is not the same as a conductor. It is not lower than every insulator, and not higher than every conductor.","trap":"Think of the middle of the scale.","answer":"between that of conductors and that of insulators"},"phy-11-10-m02":{"idea":"The size of the energy gap decides whether a solid is a conductor, semiconductor or insulator.","steps":["In a conductor the valence and conduction bands overlap, so the gap is zero.","A semiconductor has a small gap, about 1 eV.","An insulator has a large gap, for example about 5 eV for diamond."],"others":"The other three statements swap the sizes of the gaps or claim that all solids have the same gap.","trap":"Insulator: large gap. Semiconductor: small gap. Conductor: no gap.","answer":"large for an insulator, small for a semiconductor, and zero for a conductor"},"phy-11-10-m03":{"idea":"Few electrons can cross a gap of 1.12 eV at room temperature. Practically none can cross 5.47 eV.","steps":["Heat can lift some electrons across a small gap.","Silicon has a gap of 1.12 eV, so a few electrons cross at 300 K.","Diamond has 5.47 eV, so practically none cross."],"others":"They do not conduct equally. Silicon does conduct a little, so 'neither conducts' is wrong. A wider gap makes diamond a worse conductor, not a better one.","trap":"Wider gap means fewer carriers.","answer":"silicon conducts a little, because heat lifts a few electrons across the gap, but diamond does not"},"phy-11-10-m04":{"idea":"The resistance of a semiconductor falls when it is heated.","steps":["Heating breaks more bonds.","More electron-hole pairs are formed.","More carriers means more current for the same voltage, so resistance falls."],"others":"A rise would happen in a metal. The resistance does not become infinite, and it does not stay the same.","trap":"Metals: resistance rises with temperature. Semiconductors: it falls.","answer":"decreases, as more electron-hole pairs are formed"},"phy-11-10-m05":{"idea":"In a pure (intrinsic) semiconductor every free electron leaves one hole behind.","steps":["A broken bond gives one free electron.","It also leaves one hole.","So the numbers are equal: n_e = n_h."],"others":"More holes or more electrons would need doping. No carriers at all happens only at absolute zero.","trap":"Doping makes one kind more numerous.","answer":"equal numbers of free electrons and holes"},"phy-11-10-m06":{"idea":"A hole is a missing electron in a bond, and it acts like a positive charge.","steps":["When an electron leaves a bond, a gap remains.","A neighbour can fill it, and the gap moves to the neighbour's place.","The gap moves opposite to the electron and acts like a charge +e."],"others":"It is not a positive ion, and not a light free electron. It is also not an empty place with missing atoms.","trap":"Holes are not particles. They are the absence of an electron.","answer":"the absence of an electron in a covalent bond, and it behaves as a positive charge"},"phy-11-10-m07":{"idea":"An n-type semiconductor needs an impurity with extra electrons.","steps":["Silicon has four valence electrons.","Phosphorus or arsenic has five.","Four bond with silicon and the fifth is almost free, so electrons become the majority carriers."],"others":"A trivalent impurity (boron, aluminium) makes p-type. Tetravalent atoms add no extra carriers. Noble gases do not dope silicon.","trap":"n-type: pentavalent (five). p-type: trivalent (three).","answer":"a pentavalent impurity, such as phosphorus or arsenic"},"phy-11-10-m08":{"idea":"In p-type material holes are the majority carriers.","steps":["A trivalent dopant has only three valence electrons.","It leaves a hole in one bond, so there are many holes.","A few electrons are still present from heat, so electrons are the minority carriers."],"others":"Electrons as the majority belong to n-type. 'No minority carriers' is wrong, because heat always creates some pairs.","trap":"Majority and minority carriers are both present in each type.","answer":"holes, and the minority carriers are electrons"},"phy-11-10-m09":{"idea":"Doping does not charge the crystal. It stays neutral overall.","steps":["The impurity atoms are neutral, and so is the pure crystal.","A donor gives up an electron and becomes a fixed positive ion.","The positive ions balance the extra electrons, so the total charge is zero."],"others":"It is not positively charged, because the fixed ions are balanced by free electrons. It is not negatively charged for the same reason. It does have free charges.","trap":"n-type means many electrons, not negative charge.","answer":"electrically neutral, since the donor ions have a positive charge that balances the extra electrons"},"phy-11-10-m10":{"idea":"A tiny amount of doping changes conductivity enormously.","steps":["A pure crystal has very few carriers at room temperature.","Each dopant atom adds one carrier.","Even one atom per million gives far more carriers than the pure crystal, so conductivity rises by a factor of thousands."],"others":"A small fraction or exactly one part per million would ignore how few carriers a pure crystal has. 'Not at all' is wrong.","trap":"Small amount does not mean small effect.","answer":"by a very large factor"},"phy-11-10-m11":{"idea":"The depletion region is the thin layer at a p-n junction with no free carriers.","steps":["Electrons from the n side and holes from the p side cross and recombine.","This leaves fixed positive donor ions and negative acceptor ions.","The layer has no free carriers, and the ions create an electric field."],"others":"It is not the region with only holes and no field. It is not far from the junction. It does not have a large number of free carriers.","trap":"Depleted means emptied of carriers.","answer":"has no free charge carriers, and has an electric field in it"},"phy-11-10-m12":{"idea":"Forward bias means the battery pushes carriers towards the junction. The p side goes to the positive terminal.","steps":["The p side is connected to the positive terminal.","The applied voltage opposes the built-in barrier.","The barrier is lowered and a large current flows."],"others":"The p side on the negative terminal is reverse bias. n joined to the positive terminal is also reverse bias. Both sides to the same terminal gives no bias.","trap":"Forward: p to +, n to −.","answer":"the p side is joined to the positive terminal of the battery"},"phy-11-10-m13":{"idea":"Reverse bias widens the depletion region and blocks the current.","steps":["The reverse voltage pulls carriers away from the junction.","More fixed ions are uncovered, so the depletion region widens.","The barrier is higher, and only a tiny leakage current flows."],"others":"A narrower region with a large current is forward bias. A narrower region with a tiny current does not match. A fixed width with a large current does not happen.","trap":"Reverse: wider region, tiny current.","answer":"increases, and only a very small current flows"},"phy-11-10-m14":{"idea":"An ideal diode is a perfect one-way switch.","steps":["In forward bias it conducts freely, so the resistance is zero.","In reverse bias it blocks completely, so the resistance is infinite.","That is the ideal behaviour."],"others":"Zero resistance both ways would be a plain wire. Equal resistance both ways is a resistor. Infinite forward and zero reverse is the opposite of a diode.","trap":"A real diode only approaches this.","answer":"zero resistance in forward bias and infinite resistance in reverse bias"},"phy-11-10-m15":{"idea":"A silicon diode starts to conduct strongly at about 0.7 V in forward bias.","steps":["Below the knee voltage the forward current is tiny.","Above it, the current rises quickly.","For silicon the knee is about 0.7 V (for germanium about 0.3 V)."],"others":"0.3 V is germanium. 7 V and 1.5 V are not typical knee voltages.","trap":"Silicon 0.7 V, germanium 0.3 V.","answer":"0.7 V"},"phy-11-10-m16":{"idea":"Dynamic resistance is the change of voltage for a small change of current.","steps":["Static resistance is V/I at one point.","Dynamic resistance uses small changes: r = ΔV/ΔI.","It is the reciprocal of the slope of the I–V curve."],"others":"V × I is power, not resistance. V/I is the static resistance. ΔI/ΔV is the slope itself (a conductance).","trap":"Slope gives conductance, its reciprocal gives resistance.","answer":"r = ΔV/ΔI, the reciprocal of the slope of the I–V curve at that point"},"phy-11-10-m17":{"idea":"Semiconductor devices are small, efficient and instant, unlike vacuum tubes.","steps":["They have no filament to heat, so they start at once.","They need little power and a low voltage.","They can be made very small and put together in integrated circuits."],"others":"The other options describe vacuum tubes: large, hungry for power, or needing a warm-up.","trap":"No warm-up time is the key advantage.","answer":"smaller, use less power, and start working at once"},"phy-11-10-m18":{"idea":"The weak points of semiconductors are heat sensitivity and limited power.","steps":["Their properties change with temperature.","A large current heats the device too much.","So they are sensitive to temperature and cannot handle very high power."],"others":"Large size, a heated filament and a very high operating voltage are faults of vacuum tubes.","trap":"Old tubes: bulky, hot filament, high voltage.","answer":"they are sensitive to temperature and cannot handle very high power"},"phy-11-10-m19":{"idea":"A solar cell turns light into electricity.","steps":["Light with enough energy makes electron-hole pairs near a p-n junction.","The junction field separates the pairs.","A voltage and a current appear."],"others":"Heat to sound, chemical storage and electrical to light are other devices. An LED is the opposite of a solar cell.","trap":"Solar cell: light to electricity. LED: electricity to light.","answer":"converts light energy into electrical energy"},"phy-11-10-m20":{"idea":"An LED must be forward biased to emit light.","steps":["In forward bias, electrons and holes meet at the junction.","They recombine and release energy.","The energy comes out as photons, so the diode glows."],"others":"In reverse bias there is almost no current. With no battery nothing flows. Heat is not the cause of the light.","trap":"The colour depends on the energy gap of the material.","answer":"forward biased"},"phy-11-10-m21":{"idea":"An NTC thermistor has a resistance that falls when it gets hotter.","steps":["It behaves like a pure semiconductor.","More temperature gives more carriers.","More carriers lowers the resistance."],"others":"Resistance falling when temperature falls is the opposite. Rising with temperature is a PTC. Independence from temperature would not be a thermistor.","trap":"NTC: negative temperature coefficient, resistance goes down as temperature goes up.","answer":"the resistance falls when the temperature rises"},"phy-11-10-m22":{"idea":"A PTC thermistor works as a resettable fuse because its resistance jumps up when it gets hot.","steps":["Too much current heats the thermistor.","Its resistance rises sharply.","The current drops. When it cools, resistance falls and the circuit resets."],"others":"A falling resistance would raise the current further. A fixed resistance gives no protection. Zero resistance would not stop the current.","trap":"PTC: resistance rises with temperature.","answer":"rises sharply when it is heated by too large a current, which cuts down the current"},"phy-11-10-n01":{"idea":"A photon can lift an electron across the gap only if its energy is at least the gap energy.","steps":["Longest wavelength means the smallest energy that works: E = 1.12 eV.","E = 1.12 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.792 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","λ = hc/E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/1.792 × 10⁻¹⁹.","λ = 1.11 × 10⁻⁶ m = 1110 nm (infrared)."],"trap":"A longer wavelength has less energy and cannot cross the gap.","answer":"1110 nm"},"phy-11-10-n02":{"idea":"In a semiconductor the product of electron and hole numbers stays at n_i².","steps":["The law of mass action is n_e × n_h = n_i².","n_i² = (1.5 × 10¹⁶)² = 2.25 × 10³².","n_h = 2.25 × 10³²/5.0 × 10²² = 4.5 × 10⁹ per m³."],"trap":"More electrons from doping means far fewer holes.","answer":"4.5 (in units of 10⁹ per m³)"},"phy-11-10-n03":{"idea":"Dynamic resistance is a slope: change in voltage over change in current.","steps":["ΔV = 0.74 − 0.70 = 0.04 V.","ΔI = 24 − 14 = 10 mA = 0.010 A.","r = ΔV/ΔI = 0.04/0.010 = 4.0 Ω."],"trap":"Convert mA to A. The static resistance (V/I) is a different number, 50 Ω at 0.70 V.","answer":"4.0 Ω"},"phy-11-10-n04":{"idea":"A thermistor's resistance changes with temperature through an exponential.","steps":["Find 1/T − 1/T₀ = 1/308.15 − 1/298.15 = −1.0884 × 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹.","Multiply by β = 3000: the exponent is −0.3265.","R = 10.0 × e^(−0.3265) = 10.0 × 0.7214.","R = 7.21 kΩ."],"trap":"The resistance falls as the temperature rises, as for an NTC thermistor.","answer":"7.21 kΩ"},"phy-11-10-n05":{"idea":"Wavelength comes from photon energy: λ = hc/E.","steps":["E = 2.0 eV = 2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","hc = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵ J m.","λ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.22 × 10⁻⁷ m.","λ = 622 nm, which is red."],"trap":"Convert eV to joules first.","answer":"622 nm"},"phy-11-10-n06":{"idea":"Use Ohm's law on the resistor, after subtracting the diode's voltage drop.","steps":["Battery voltage is 5.0 V and the diode drops 0.70 V.","Voltage across the resistor = 5.0 − 0.70 = 4.30 V.","I = V/R = 4.30/430 = 0.0100 A.","I = 10.00 mA."],"trap":"A conducting silicon diode keeps about 0.7 V across it.","answer":"10.00 mA"}};
